# Backend Rules - Slack Standup Bot

## 1. Backend Architecture

### 1.1 Server Configuration
- Use Express.js as the web framework
- Implement proper middleware for parsing request bodies
- Set appropriate timeouts for long-running operations
- Use compression for response payloads

### 1.2 Route Organization
- Group routes by functionality (commands, events, interact)
- Keep route handlers thin - delegate to service functions
- Use async/await for asynchronous operations
- Return appropriate HTTP status codes

## 2. Slack API Integration

### 2.1 API Client
- All Slack API calls must go through the slackClient service
- Handle rate limiting appropriately
- Implement exponential backoff for failed requests
- Log all API errors with context

### 2.2 Event Handling
- Respond to Slack events within 3 seconds
- Process complex operations asynchronously
- Verify request signatures for all incoming requests
- Acknowledge receipt before processing

## 3. Data Management

### 3.1 Configuration Storage
- Use a structured JSON format for configuration
- Implement atomic writes to prevent corruption
- Validate schema before saving
- Implement backups for configuration data

### 3.2 State Management
- Use in-memory state sparingly
- Clear user state after standup completion
- Handle process restarts gracefully
- Implement recovery mechanisms for interrupted standups

## 4. Error Handling

### 4.1 Error Strategies
- Use try/catch blocks around async operations
- Prefer the await-to-js pattern for error handling
- Create custom error classes for different error types
- Provide contextual information in error messages

### 4.2 Logging
- Use structured logging (JSON format)
- Include request IDs in logs
- Log appropriate level (info, warn, error)
- Avoid logging sensitive information

## 5. Performance Optimization

### 5.1 Response Time
- Respond to Slack events within 3 seconds
- Use asynchronous processing for time-consuming tasks
- Implement caching for frequently accessed data
- Monitor response times in production

### 5.2 Resource Usage
- Optimize scheduled job frequency
- Use worker processes for CPU-intensive tasks
- Implement connection pooling
- Close unused connections

## 6. Security

### 6.1 Input Validation
- Validate all input from Slack
- Sanitize user inputs before processing
- Implement request timeouts
- Use parameterized queries where applicable

### 6.2 Authentication & Authorization
- Verify Slack request signatures
- Store tokens securely
- Implement token rotation
- Validate team ID in multi-team contexts

## 7. Standup Scheduling

### 7.1 Scheduling Requirements
- Use node-schedule for cron-like scheduling
- Schedule based on user timezone
- Handle daylight saving time transitions
- Implement limits on concurrent standups

### 7.2 Timezone Handling
- Retrieve user timezones from Slack profiles
- Schedule jobs in the user's local time
- Use a default timezone as fallback
- Update schedules when user timezone changes

## 8. Message Formatting

### 8.1 Message Structure
- Use Block Kit for rich message formatting
- Ensure mobile compatibility
- Follow Slack message formatting guidelines
- Implement accessibility best practices

### 8.2 Thread Management
- Post standup summaries as parent messages
- Reply to summary with individual answers
- Maintain thread structure for readability
- Include user mentions for visibility

## 9. Testing Strategy

### 9.1 Unit Testing
- Test all service functions
- Mock external dependencies
- Test error handling paths
- Aim for high code coverage

### 9.2 Integration Testing
- Test route handlers with mock requests
- Verify correct interaction with services
- Test configuration persistence
- Test scheduling functionality

## 10. Code Style

### 10.1 Formatting
- Use Prettier for consistent formatting
- Use ESLint with appropriate rule set
- Maintain consistent indentation (2 spaces)
- Limit line length to 100 characters

### 10.2 Function Design
- Keep functions small and focused
- Use descriptive parameter names
- Document function purpose and parameters
- Avoid side effects when possible

## 11. Dependency Management

### 11.1 Package Selection
- Prefer established, well-maintained packages
- Audit dependencies for security vulnerabilities
- Lock dependency versions
- Document purpose of each dependency

### 11.2 Dependency Updates
- Regularly update dependencies
- Test thoroughly after updates
- Update one dependency at a time
- Document breaking changes

## 12. Environment-specific Configuration

### 12.1 Development Environment
- Use nodemon for automatic restarts
- Configure verbose logging
- Use dotenv for environment variables
- Enable additional debugging information

### 12.2 Production Environment
- Configure appropriate logging levels
- Implement health check endpoints
- Set up monitoring and alerting
- Use process managers (PM2, Docker) 